    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-black text-white tracking-tight mb-6">
      Cyprus 60-Day Rule & Non-Dom Tax Regime for Digital Nomads (2026 Guide)
    </h1>

    <!-- Quick Answer Box (Exact 53 words with green accent border) -->
    <div class="border-l-4 border-emerald-500 bg-emerald-950/20 border-t border-r border-b border-emerald-500/30 rounded-xl p-6 shadow-xl mb-10">
      <div class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400 font-mono mb-2">
        ⚡ Quick Answer: Cyprus 60-Day Tax Viability
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-200 leading-relaxed">
        The Cyprus 60-day rule provides the fastest path to legal European tax residency for digital nomads without requiring 183 days in-country. Qualifying under Non-Domicile status unlocks a 0% tax rate on worldwide dividends and capital gains for 17 years, paired with 2.65% mandatory healthcare contributions (GESY), making it superior to Portugal or Spain.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Article Content -->
    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-slate-300 space-y-6 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        International tax compliance for high-earning location-independent professionals, digital nomads, and remote software founders is frequently complicated by the rigid 183-day rule observed by traditional tax authorities. Spending six continuous months in a single jurisdiction is often impractical for entrepreneurs whose operational footprint spans multiple countries.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recognizing this reality, the Republic of Cyprus enacted the revolutionary <strong>60-day tax residency rule</strong> (amending Section 2 of the Cyprus Income Tax Law). Combined with the country's British common-law legal foundation, widespread use of English in commerce and courts, and a territorial-leaning non-domicile regime, Cyprus has established itself as the European Union's premier domicile of choice for remote founders.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">1. The Five Statutory Conditions of the Cyprus 60-Day Rule</h2>
      <p>
        To legally claim tax residency in Cyprus under the 60-day rule and obtain a certified Tax Residency Certificate (TRC) recognized by international banks and foreign tax authorities under bilateral Double Taxation Treaties (DTAs), an individual must satisfy five strict cumulative conditions during a single calendar tax year (January 1 to December 31):
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Condition 1 (Physical Stay):</strong> Remain in Cyprus for at least 60 days in the tax year. Days are calculated using airline passenger manifests and border entry/exit stamps.</li>
        <li><strong>Condition 2 (No Secondary Dominance):</strong> Do not spend more than 183 days in any other single foreign jurisdiction during that same tax year.</li>
        <li><strong>Condition 3 (No Foreign Tax Residence):</strong> Not be deemed a tax resident under the domestic tax legislation of any other sovereign state.</li>
        <li><strong>Condition 4 (Permanent Residential Home):</strong> Maintain a permanent residential property in Cyprus, either through freehold title ownership or a continuous registered long-term lease agreement.</li>
        <li><strong>Condition 5 (Economic Tie):</strong> Carry out any business in Cyprus, be employed in Cyprus, or hold the office of Director in a Cyprus tax-resident company as of December 31 of the tax year.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">2. Comparative Matrix: Cyprus Non-Dom vs Portugal IFICI vs Spain Beckham Law</h2>
      <p>
        Here is how the Cyprus 60-day regime benchmarks against alternative Mediterranean nomad tax options:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/80 mb-8 p-1 shadow-2xl">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300">
