      </div>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">3. Understanding the Non-Domicile (Non-Dom) Exemption</h2>
      <p>
        In Cyprus tax jurisprudence, individuals are categorized based on domicile. An individual who does not have a "domicile of origin" in Cyprus (meaning they were not born to a Cypriot father) is classified as a non-domiciled tax resident. Non-domiciled status remains active for <strong>17 out of 20 consecutive tax years</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The central advantage of non-dom status is the total exemption from the <strong>Special Defence Contribution (SDC)</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Dividends:</strong> Ordinarily subject to 17% SDC for domiciled Cypriots. For non-doms, the SDC rate is <strong>0%</strong>. This applies to both local Cyprus companies and foreign subsidiaries.</li>
        <li><strong>Passive Interest:</strong> Bank deposit interest and bond coupons are ordinarily subject to 17% SDC. For non-doms, the SDC rate is <strong>0%</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Rental Income:</strong> Domiciled residents pay 3% SDC (on 75% of gross rent). Non-doms pay 0% SDC, paying only standard personal income tax if applicable.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">4. GESY Healthcare Contributions & Annual Ceilings</h2>
      <p>
        While non-doms are entirely exempt from the SDC, all Cyprus tax residents are subject to contributions toward the General Healthcare System (GESY). GESY was introduced to fund universal healthcare across the island.
      </p>
      <p>
        The GESY contribution on dividend and passive income is <strong>2.65%</strong>. However, GESY is subject to an annual statutory ceiling: contributions are levied only up to €180,000 of aggregate income per taxpayer per year.
      </p>
      <p>
        Therefore, an entrepreneur drawing €500,000 in dividends pays 2.65% only on the first €180,000, resulting in an absolute maximum GESY contribution of <strong>€4,770 per year</strong>. On €500,000 of income, this equates to an effective tax rate of less than 0.96%.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">5. Establishing Economic Substance in Cyprus: The Holding Company Route</h2>
      <p>
        To satisfy Condition 5 of the 60-day rule, remote founders routinely incorporate a private limited liability company in Cyprus (a Cyprus Ltd). A Cyprus company offers:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li>A flat corporate income tax rate of <strong>12.5%</strong> on net profits (one of the lowest in the EU).</li>
        <li>Full participation exemption on dividends received from foreign qualifying subsidiaries.</li>
        <li>0% withholding tax on outbound dividend and interest payments to non-resident and non-dom shareholders.</li>
        <li>Intellectual Property (IP) Box regime offering an effective corporate tax rate as low as <strong>2.5%</strong> for qualifying patent and software assets.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">6. Computational Formula for Cyprus Effective Tax Rate</h2>
      <p>
        The following calculation demonstrates how to determine total tax liabilities for a Cyprus non-dom nomad drawing mixed salary and dividend income:
      </p>

      <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 my-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-200 overflow-x-auto shadow-2xl">
        <pre is:raw><code>// Cyprus Non-Dom Tax Computation Formula
function calculateCyprusTax(annualSalary, annualDividends) {
  // 1. Personal Income Tax on Salary (First €19,500 tax-free)
  let pit = 0;
  if (annualSalary > 19500) pit += (Math.min(annualSalary, 28000) - 19500) * 0.20;
  if (annualSalary > 28000) pit += (Math.min(annualSalary, 36300) - 28000) * 0.25;
  if (annualSalary > 36300) pit += (Math.min(annualSalary, 60000) - 36300) * 0.30;
  if (annualSalary > 60000) pit += (annualSalary - 60000) * 0.35;

  // 2. Special Defence Contribution (SDC) on Dividends = 0% for Non-Doms
  const sdc = 0;

  // 3. GESY Healthcare Contribution (2.65% capped at €180,000 income = €4,770 max)
  const totalIncome = annualSalary + annualDividends;
  const gesyBase = Math.min(totalIncome, 180000);
  const gesy = gesyBase * 0.0265;

  const totalTax = pit + sdc + gesy;
  const effectiveRate = (totalTax / totalIncome) * 100;

  return {
    personalIncomeTax: pit,
    sdcDividendTax: sdc,
    gesyHealthcare: gesy,
    totalTaxPaid: totalTax,
    effectiveRatePercent: effectiveRate.toFixed(2)
  };
}

// Example: €30,000 salary + €120,000 dividends
console.log(calculateCyprusTax(30000, 120000));
// Output: totalTaxPaid: €6,175 | effectiveRatePercent: 4.12%</code></pre>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-8 mb-3">7. Practical Step-by-Step Filing Workflow</h2>
      <p>
        The standard path to executing Cyprus non-dom tax residency follows these milestones:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Month 1:</strong> Secure a residential rental property (minimum 12 months) in Limassol, Larnaca, or Paphos. Register the lease with the Tax Department.</li>
        <li><strong>Month 2:</strong> Register for the MEU1 Yellow Slip (for EU nationals) or the Digital Nomad Visa (for non-EU nationals). Obtain your Tax Identification Code (TIC).</li>
        <li><strong>Month 3:</strong> File Form TD38 (Declaration for Non-Domiciled Status) with the Cyprus Tax Department accompanied by your passport, birth certificate, and proof of non-Cypriot domicile of origin.</li>
        <li><strong>Month 4+:</strong> Accumulate 60 physical days on the island, retain all boarding passes, and file for your official Tax Residency Certificate (Form TD126).</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

